# VM-Acme: Virtual Machine Generator for Vocational Training and University

![Version](https://img.shields.io/badge/version-1.9.0-blue.svg)
![Field](https://img.shields.io/badge/purpose-Educational/Systems/Creative-green.svg)

This tool allows creating customized (Debian/Ubuntu/Kali/Parrot/MX Linux/Linux Mint/LMDE) virtual machine images fully automated. It is a pedagogical and professional resource designed to democratize access to technology, ideal for those seeking a powerful and free alternative to proprietary systems.

## 🎯 User Profiles

*   **IT, Programming, Cybersecurity and Microinformatics Students:**
    *   **Initiation (SMR/ASIR):** Learn how an operating system is built from its foundations. The script is an open lesson on partitioning, mounts and package management.
    *   **Network Labs:** Generate complete infrastructures in seconds to practice routing, firewalling or pentesting.
    *   **Automation (DAW/DAM):** Study the source code to master Bash and infrastructure as code (IaC).
*   **Windows Migrants (Home and Office):**
    *   **Safe Transition:** Don't break your main computer. Create virtual machines to learn Linux, its terminal and its freedom philosophy without risks.
    *   **Total Privacy:** Forget data tracking and forced updates that slow down your work.
*   **Creatives (Design, Illustration, Video and Streaming):**
    *   **Affordable Alternative:** If you cannot afford monthly Adobe subscriptions or your hardware does not support Windows 11, Linux is your salvation.
    *   **Old Hardware:** Give a second life to that equipment Windows considers "obsolete". Linux flies where other systems crawl.
    *   **Professional Software:** Use **Krita** for illustration, **Blender** for 3D, **Kdenlive** for video editing and **OBS Studio** for your broadcasts.

## 🚀 Key Features (v1.9.0)

*   **Anti-Blocking `policy-rc.d` System:** Guarantees that installation never stops because of rebel services (like `ModemManager`). **Absolute stability in Hyper-V and virtualized environments**. Features **color-coded logging** for better visual feedback during execution (Red for errors, Yellow for warnings, Green for info, Cyan for debug).
*   **Smart Dependency Management:** Detection of virtual packages and fallback providers (e.g.: `qemu-user-binfmt`). Host dependency failures are non-fatal.
*   **Professional Multi-Format:** Support for **VirtualBox**, **VMware**, **QEMU**, **RAW**, **RootFS**, **Vagrant** and **Hyper-V**.
*   **QEMU Support:** Converts RAW images to QCOW2 format for efficient virtual machine storage and KVM/libvirt compatibility.
*   **Vagrant Support:** Creates Vagrant boxes with metadata.json and Vagrantfile for the libvirt/KVM provider.

*   **Intelligent Snap Installation:** Automatic detection of `--classic` requirements for creative software.
*   **Mirror Caching:** Caches the fastest mirror found to speed up subsequent runs.
*   **Parallel Mirror Testing:** Finds the fastest mirror through parallel network testing, reducing selection time by approximately 70%.
*   **Legacy Ubuntu Support:** Allows creating Ubuntu virtual machines even from versions that no longer have official support. The script lets you specify a custom codename and issues a warning if the version has no official support.
*   **Debian Version Selection:** Choose between the current stable release, bookworm, bullseye, buster, testing or sid.
*   **Kali Linux Support:** Create virtual machines with Kali Linux, the leading penetration testing distribution. Includes installation type selection (Default, Large, Everything, Headless, Core and **Purple** — the defensive variant with detection, response and recovery tooling) and desktop environments (XFCE, GNOME, KDE, MATE, LXDE, i3 and Enlightenment).
*   **Parrot Security Support:** Create virtual machines of the **Parrot OS** security suite. Includes the **Security** edition (full offensive toolkit, default), **Home**, **Core** and **Hack The Box**, with KDE, MATE, XFCE, GNOME and i3 desktops. The official Parrot keyring is downloaded and installed automatically.
*   **Official Kali and Parrot Keyrings:** The script fetches and integrates the official signing keys of these Debian derivatives (both in `debootstrap` and in the guest system) so that APT works without warnings.
*   **MX Linux Support:** Create virtual machines of **MX Linux** (based on Debian Stable, currently _trixie_), one of the most popular and lightweight distributions available. Includes the **XFCE** (default), **KDE Plasma**, **Fluxbox** and **AHS** (Advanced Hardware Support) editions, with XFCE, KDE, Fluxbox, MATE, GNOME, Cinnamon and LXQt desktops. It automatically adds the community repository (mxrepo.com), downloads and integrates the official `mx25-archive-keyring`, and deploys MX's own tools and applications (`mx-system`, `mx-tools`, `mx-welcome`, `mx25-artwork`, `mx-apps`).
*   **Official MX Linux Keyring:** Downloads the `mx25-archive-keyring` package, extracts the signing key and integrates it into the guest system (`/etc/apt/trusted.gpg.d` and `/usr/share/keyrings`) to validate the mxrepo.com repository.
*   **Linux Mint Support (`mint`):** Create virtual machines of **Linux Mint** (Ubuntu 24.04 _noble_ base), the most popular and newcomer-friendly desktop distribution. Includes the **Cinnamon** (default), **MATE** and **XFCE** editions. It automatically adds the official Mint repository (`packages.linuxmint.com`) alongside Ubuntu's, downloads and integrates the `linuxmint-keyring`, and deploys the `mint-meta-core`, `mint-meta-codecs` and `mint-meta-<desktop>` metapackages.
*   **Linux Mint Debian Edition Support (`lmde`):** Create virtual machines of **LMDE 7 "Gigi"** (Debian 13 _trixie_ base), the Linux Mint variant with no Ubuntu dependency. Includes the **Cinnamon** (default) edition and the option of XFCE/MATE through the Debian tasks. Adds the Mint repository, its keyring and Mint's base packages.
*   **Network support for Linux Mint and LMDE:** Like MX Linux, the Mint editions install and configure **NetworkManager** to manage networking from first boot.

## 📘 Beginner and Professional Guide

### 🎓 IT Students
- **Cybersecurity:** Create "victim" and "attacker" machines with isolated network configurations.
- **Microinformatics:** Observe how `debootstrap` installs only what is necessary. The base for understanding "informatic minimalism".

### 🪟 If you come from Windows
- **Software Equivalents:** Use **LibreOffice** for Office, **GIMP** for Photoshop and **Inkscape** for Illustrator. Most are free and open source.
- **Safe Installation:** Here you don't download strange ".exe" files. Everything comes from community-verified repositories.

### 🎨 Creatives and Streamers
- **Illustration:** Connect your graphics tablet (Wacom, Huion, XP-Pen) and open **Krita**. Pressure and color management is amazing.
- **Video Editing:** **Kdenlive** allows 4K editing with proxies, ideal if your equipment has few resources.
- **Streaming:** **OBS Studio** on Linux consumes less CPU, allowing your games or design applications to run better while you broadcast.

## 🛠️ Quick Start Guide

```bash
# Example: Create a machine for a networking student
sudo ./create_vm.sh --name lab_redes --os debian --ram 1024 --desktop none --apt nmap,tcpdump

# Example: Create a station for a digital illustrator
sudo ./create_vm.sh --name estudio_arte --os ubuntu --desktop xfce --apt krita,gimp,inkscape

# Example: Create a pentesting lab with Parrot Security
sudo ./create_vm.sh --name lab_pentest --os parrot --edition security --desktop kde --ram 4096

# Example: Create a lightweight workstation with MX Linux
sudo ./create_vm.sh --name mx_workstation --os mx --edition xfce --desktop mx-xfce --ram 2048

# Example: Create a Linux Mint (Cinnamon) desktop to migrate from Windows
sudo ./create_vm.sh --name mint_workstation --os mint --desktop mint-cinnamon --ram 3072

# Example: Create a LMDE 7 (Debian Edition) desktop with Cinnamon
sudo ./create_vm.sh --name lmde_workstation --os lmde --desktop lmde-cinnamon --ram 3072

# Example: Create a defensive lab with Kali Linux Purple
sudo ./create_vm.sh --name purple_lab --os kali --edition purple --desktop kali-xfce --ram 4096
```

## 📚 Bibliography and Learning Resources

### 🛠️ Technology and Systems
1.  **Debian Administrator's Handbook:** The definitive guide to understanding the system. [debian-handbook.info](https://debian-handbook.info/browse/stable/).
2.  **Linux Journey:** A structured course from the most basic to server administration. [linuxjourney.com](https://linuxjourney.com/).
3.  **Vagrant Docs:** Learn to automate your test laboratories. [vagrantup.com/docs](https://www.vagrantup.com/docs).
4.  **QEMU Documentation:** Understand how hardware emulation works. [qemu.org/docs](https://qemu.org/docs/master/).

### 🎨 Multimedia Creation and Design
5.  **Krita Manual:** Professional manual for digital artists. [docs.krita.org](https://docs.krita.org/).
6.  **Ubuntu Studio Manual:** Guide to setting up a high-level multimedia production system. [ubuntustudio.org/manual/](https://ubuntustudio.org/manual/).
7.  **Blender Fundamentals:** Learn the industry standard for 3D. [docs.blender.org](https://docs.blender.org/manual/en/latest/).
8.  **Kdenlive User Manual:** Professional video editing step by step. [docs.kdenlive.org](https://docs.kdenlive.org/).

### 🐧 Transition and Digital Culture
9.  **AlternativeTo Linux:** Find the best free alternatives to your Windows programs. [alternativeto.net](https://alternativeto.net/platform/linux/).
10. **It's FOSS:** The best blog for beginners who want practical advice. [itsfoss.com](https://itsfoss.com/).
11. **DistroSea:** Try Linux in your browser without installing anything. [distrosea.com](https://distrosea.com/).
12. **Free Software Foundation (FSF):** Learn about the ethical importance of free software. [fsf.org](https://www.fsf.org/).

